1
00:00:15,848 --> 00:00:17,725
Jane? You in there?

2
00:00:18,726 --> 00:00:21,395
- I'm up. I'm up.
- Homicide in San Francisco.

3
00:00:21,521 --> 00:00:23,356
Cho and Rigsby are already
on their way.

4
00:00:24,065 --> 00:00:25,107
Did you sleep here?

5
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:26,526
No, I was working.

6
00:00:26,859 --> 00:00:28,361
It looks like you were sleeping.

7
00:00:28,986 --> 00:00:32,281
Well, I programmed my, uh, dreams
to work on the Red John case.

8
00:00:32,406 --> 00:00:34,408
Oh, well, then make sure
you put in for overtime.

9
00:00:34,617 --> 00:00:37,411
Ah, healthy skepticism.
I like it. But it could work.

10
00:00:37,537 --> 00:00:41,249
Mendeleev saw the entire
periodic table in his dream.

11
00:00:41,457 --> 00:00:42,625
That's nice for him.

12
00:00:43,084 --> 00:00:44,794
- Jane? As a friend...
- Shall we?

13
00:00:46,003 --> 00:00:47,046
This doesn't look good.

14
00:00:50,424 --> 00:00:51,467
Keeps me going.

15
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:01,477
"There's evidence
of a physical confrontation"

16
00:01:01,852 --> 00:01:02,937
and this is where
he ended up.

17
00:01:03,396 --> 00:01:04,814
He was on his back
when the shots were fired.

18
00:01:04,939 --> 00:01:06,315
Eight to ten rounds to the head.

19
00:01:06,691 --> 00:01:07,900
- What head?
- Yeah.

20
00:01:09,068 --> 00:01:11,320
Two motel guests heard
muffled pops around 1:00 A.M.

21
00:01:11,445 --> 00:01:12,530
figured it was city traffic.

22
00:01:12,905 --> 00:01:15,324
No defense wounds. You'd think
he would've at least put up his hands

23
00:01:15,449 --> 00:01:17,410
- to shield his face.
- Well, it depends how hard he fell.

24
00:01:17,618 --> 00:01:19,704
Forensics recovered the casings.
9 millimeter.

25
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:21,080
Also collected a cigarette butt

26
00:01:21,247 --> 00:01:22,331
and some red fibers
around the body.

27
00:01:22,498 --> 00:01:24,041
You woke me for this?

28
00:01:25,626 --> 00:01:27,837
This man is a sociopath.
He should've been in prison.

29
00:01:28,045 --> 00:01:30,089
He was.
Full pardon two days ago.

30
00:01:30,506 --> 00:01:32,258
Name's Henry Dahl.
Convicted nine years ago

31
00:01:32,383 --> 00:01:34,010
in the rape and murder
of one Jennie Winter.

32
00:01:34,468 --> 00:01:37,179
Was a lifer at Del Norte
until a new DNA test set him free.

33
00:01:37,638 --> 00:01:39,473
- How'd you peg him as an inmate?
- Give me a break.

34
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:42,435
A classic rooster build...
massive upper body, skinny legs.

35
00:01:42,602 --> 00:01:44,729
Jaundiced skin
probably hepatitis C

36
00:01:44,854 --> 00:01:46,731
from the dirty needle tattoo
on his elbow.

37
00:01:46,981 --> 00:01:48,983
Spiderweb representing
the passage of time.

38
00:01:49,108 --> 00:01:50,192
Wh-where are you going?

39
00:01:50,735 --> 00:01:54,822
I'm tired, and I don't really care
who killed this creep.

40
00:01:54,989 --> 00:01:56,032
He was innocent.

41
00:01:57,158 --> 00:01:59,577
He made instruments
of death out of toothpicks.

42
00:02:12,214 --> 00:02:13,382
I know you, don't I?

43
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:15,718
Are you with homicide?

44
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:18,679
Right now I'm with you
unless, uh, of course...

45
00:02:18,971 --> 00:02:20,014
Are you with homicide?

46
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:22,933
Did you know it cost taxpayers
2.4 million

47
00:02:23,059 --> 00:02:24,852
to convict Henry Dahl
in the murder of Jennie Winter?

48
00:02:24,977 --> 00:02:26,062
No kidding.
That's a lot of money.

49
00:02:26,187 --> 00:02:28,731
Now they'll have to spend millions
more finding and convicting Dahl's killer

50
00:02:28,856 --> 00:02:30,441
because the state set
a hardened criminal free.

51
00:02:30,608 --> 00:02:33,402
How many man hours will you waste
chasing Henry Dahl's killer?

52
00:02:33,611 --> 00:02:34,654
Not too many.

53
00:02:36,947 --> 00:02:39,116
- Anything usable?
- Eh... maybe.

54
00:02:54,632 --> 00:02:55,675
Get this.

55
00:03:16,485 --> 00:03:20,155
"And I'm here to tell you
Henry Dahl deserved what he got."

56
00:03:20,322 --> 00:03:22,366
"Even the CBI agrees with me... "

57
00:03:22,533 --> 00:03:24,910
"the premiere law enforcement
agency of California."

58
00:03:25,119 --> 00:03:26,412
"Take a look
at one of their agents"

59
00:03:26,537 --> 00:03:29,748
"just two hours after the gruesome
body was discovered"

60
00:03:29,873 --> 00:03:32,209
"in a South San Francisco
motel room."

61
00:03:32,751 --> 00:03:35,963
"Not in too big a rush
to catch the killer, is he?"

62
00:03:36,255 --> 00:03:38,716
{\an8}"We've identified this man
as Patrick Jane"

63
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:41,343
{\an8}"the CBI's top criminal consultant."

64
00:03:41,677 --> 00:03:45,347
{\an8}"Sleep tight tonight, Mr. Jane.
I know I will."

65
00:03:46,890 --> 00:03:49,643
{\an8}This isn't unfamiliar territory
to me.

66
00:03:49,768 --> 00:03:52,229
A paid consultant
mistaking state contracts

67
00:03:52,354 --> 00:03:54,940
for a country club
where he can do as he pleases.

68
00:03:55,190 --> 00:03:57,234
{\an8}He was tired
because he was here all night

69
00:03:57,359 --> 00:03:59,945
{\an8}working on the Red John case.
It's completely bogus.

70
00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:02,740
{\an8}Yeah, I know it's bogus.
But that's not how it looks.

71
00:04:03,157 --> 00:04:05,451
{\an8}It looks like he puked
in the fish tank.

72
00:04:05,701 --> 00:04:07,077
Kinda does, yeah.

73
00:04:07,453 --> 00:04:09,288
The attorney general
has enough to worry about

74
00:04:09,413 --> 00:04:11,373
with the murder
of a wrongly imprisoned man.

75
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:13,500
I need Jane awake on this.

76
00:04:13,876 --> 00:04:16,086
{\an8}To be honest, I'm more concerned
about her behavior

77
00:04:16,211 --> 00:04:17,379
{\an8}than I am about his.

78
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:20,716
The police never released
Dahl's identity to the press.

79
00:04:21,049 --> 00:04:24,094
{\an8}So what's a famous cable TV host
doing in front of the motel?

80
00:04:24,303 --> 00:04:25,679
{\an8}Karen Cross' production company

81
00:04:25,804 --> 00:04:27,765
{\an8}footed the bill
for Henry Dahl's motel room.

82
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:29,516
{\an8}Dahl had driven in from Lodi

83
00:04:29,641 --> 00:04:31,602
{\an8}to appear as a guest
on her talk show today.

84
00:04:31,727 --> 00:04:33,145
Explains how she knew
he was there.

85
00:04:33,312 --> 00:04:35,063
Wait. Their connection runs
deeper than that.

86
00:04:35,522 --> 00:04:38,859
{\an8}Before Cross joined Trial TV
she was a State Prosecutor

87
00:04:38,984 --> 00:04:41,820
with 102 violent felony
convictions, no losses.

88
00:04:42,196 --> 00:04:43,655
{\an8}Guess who one
of those convictions was?

89
00:04:43,781 --> 00:04:45,282
{\an8}Hello, Dahl-y.

90
00:04:45,657 --> 00:04:46,700
Don't do that.

91
00:04:46,867 --> 00:04:48,786
Why would Dahl appear
on the national talk show

92
00:04:48,911 --> 00:04:50,245
{\an8}of the lawyer who put him away?

93
00:04:50,704 --> 00:04:52,372
{\an8}Notoriety. Payback. Women.

94
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:54,458
Well, Cross can't be happy
about Dahl's name being cleared.

95
00:04:54,583 --> 00:04:55,709
Ruins her perfect record.

96
00:04:55,834 --> 00:04:57,669
{\an8}Agreed. Jane and I will go
talk to her.

97
00:04:57,961 --> 00:04:59,421
{\an8}Now did Dahl have
a local residence?

98
00:04:59,588 --> 00:05:00,839
A halfway house in Lodi.

99
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:03,592
{\an8}You and Cho, start driving.
See what you find there.

100
00:05:04,259 --> 00:05:05,302
I'll grab the keys.

101
00:05:10,474 --> 00:05:11,517
Hey, what's the matter?

102
00:05:13,185 --> 00:05:15,562
- I had a lunch appointment.
- Well, I can drive to Lodi with Cho.

103
00:05:15,854 --> 00:05:17,356
Oh, no. I couldn't ask you
to do that.

104
00:05:17,481 --> 00:05:18,607
{\an8}Don't worry about it.
I'll clear it with Lisbon.

105
00:05:18,732 --> 00:05:20,108
{\an8}You know, you go do
your lunch thing.

106
00:05:20,734 --> 00:05:21,777
I hope you're hungry.

107
00:05:22,903 --> 00:05:24,071
{\an8}- Hey, Grace.
- Hey.

108
00:05:24,446 --> 00:05:25,489
{\an8}- Rigsby.
- Hey.

109
00:05:25,906 --> 00:05:27,366
{\an8}So you should grab a jacket.

110
00:05:27,491 --> 00:05:29,284
{\an8}I hitched us a ride
on the Tac team chopper.

111
00:05:29,910 --> 00:05:31,036
{\an8}We're flying to lunch?

112
00:05:31,286 --> 00:05:32,538
{\an8}TAC team was heading up
north anyhow

113
00:05:32,704 --> 00:05:35,916
so they're gonna drop us off at this
great picnic spot I know on the River.

114
00:05:36,667 --> 00:05:38,001
I mean, you said you had
a couple hours, right?

115
00:05:38,168 --> 00:05:41,213
Yeah. Thank you so much.
I owe you one.

116
00:05:47,427 --> 00:05:49,680
{\an8}It's okay.
We'll stop for Big Gulps.

117
00:05:57,187 --> 00:05:58,605
{\an8}How you doing?
You all right?

118
00:05:58,897 --> 00:06:01,149
Eh, it's a little chilly
in here, no?

119
00:06:04,027 --> 00:06:06,905
Excuse me. Hi.
I'm Agent Lisbon.

120
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:09,032
I think you know my colleague
Patrick Jane.

121
00:06:09,533 --> 00:06:10,826
Well, he's probably taking a nap.

122
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:16,415
He gets nervous on TV sets.

123
00:06:16,707 --> 00:06:18,709
I imagine so, considering
what happened to his family

124
00:06:18,834 --> 00:06:20,294
after his last talk show appearance.

125
00:06:20,878 --> 00:06:23,088
Even I know better than
to taunt a serial killer on air.

126
00:06:23,922 --> 00:06:26,174
Then you should know better
than to taunt the CBI, because...

127
00:06:26,341 --> 00:06:27,759
I'm a person of interest.
I get it.

128
00:06:27,885 --> 00:06:29,386
So let me make this
easy for you.

129
00:06:29,553 --> 00:06:32,264
Yes, I was outraged
that Henry Dahl was set free.

130
00:06:32,389 --> 00:06:33,682
Did I shoot him? No.

131
00:06:33,849 --> 00:06:35,559
I was in Edit Bay 4
at the time of his murder.

132
00:06:35,684 --> 00:06:37,978
The log includes a contact number
for the editor who was with me.

133
00:06:38,145 --> 00:06:39,730
Why'd you invite Henry
on your show?

134
00:06:39,938 --> 00:06:41,231
The state may have let Henry go

135
00:06:41,356 --> 00:06:43,567
but my show is tougher
than any court of law.

136
00:06:43,692 --> 00:06:44,943
I was gonna nail him
to the cross.

137
00:06:45,235 --> 00:06:46,361
Karen Cross?

138
00:06:47,529 --> 00:06:48,572
You've seen my promos?

139
00:06:48,697 --> 00:06:50,949
Read the trial coverage, too.
It was all smoke and mirrors.

140
00:06:51,283 --> 00:06:52,618
You whipped the jury
into a frenzy

141
00:06:52,743 --> 00:06:54,286
to cover for a lack
of physical evidence.

142
00:06:54,453 --> 00:06:56,288
Two witnesses put Dahl
at the scene.

143
00:06:56,455 --> 00:06:57,956
That doesn't make him
a killer.

144
00:06:58,415 --> 00:07:00,626
You caught a break
when the DNA test from the rape kit

145
00:07:00,751 --> 00:07:02,044
came back as inconclusive.

146
00:07:02,294 --> 00:07:04,880
The new test excludes Dahl
as the perpetrator.

147
00:07:05,255 --> 00:07:06,298
You don't trust DNA?

148
00:07:06,423 --> 00:07:08,216
I don't trust anything coming
outta the justice system.

149
00:07:08,383 --> 00:07:09,426
That's why I left.

150
00:07:09,718 --> 00:07:10,928
I heard you left

151
00:07:11,094 --> 00:07:12,679
because you were gonna be fired
for ethical violations.

152
00:07:12,804 --> 00:07:15,682
Nothing stops me from following
my instincts, Agent Lisbon.

153
00:07:15,807 --> 00:07:16,892
I won't apologize for that.

154
00:07:17,017 --> 00:07:19,394
I spent six months deposing
and questioning Henry.

155
00:07:19,519 --> 00:07:21,980
I'm telling you for a fact
that man was a reptile.

156
00:07:25,817 --> 00:07:26,860
Ooh tea!

157
00:07:40,082 --> 00:07:42,000
- Shrink, right?
- Excuse me?

158
00:07:43,335 --> 00:07:44,378
The vest.

159
00:07:45,003 --> 00:07:46,046
The tea.

160
00:07:46,672 --> 00:07:48,090
Karen Cross
always invites a shrink

161
00:07:48,215 --> 00:07:49,591
to comfort the grieving mother.

162
00:07:51,426 --> 00:07:52,469
I'm Jodi Dahl.

163
00:07:53,470 --> 00:07:54,888
I'm sorry for your loss.

164
00:07:55,931 --> 00:07:57,182
Don't you worry about me.

165
00:07:58,350 --> 00:07:59,977
I've been letting go
of Henry for years.

166
00:08:01,436 --> 00:08:02,479
Excuse me, hon.

167
00:08:10,904 --> 00:08:11,947
Tea?

168
00:08:12,990 --> 00:08:15,242
It's a hug in a... cup.

169
00:08:15,492 --> 00:08:16,535
No, no, thank you.

170
00:08:17,953 --> 00:08:19,287
I take it you're family, too.

171
00:08:21,248 --> 00:08:22,290
Max Winter.

172
00:08:22,541 --> 00:08:23,583
Max Winter.

173
00:08:25,335 --> 00:08:26,586
The widower of the victim.

174
00:08:29,715 --> 00:08:30,757
True victim.

175
00:08:31,425 --> 00:08:32,718
My wife's name was Jennie.

176
00:08:36,930 --> 00:08:39,391
There is nothing about you
that indicates loss.

177
00:08:39,975 --> 00:08:42,310
No scars of aged pain.
Bravo.

178
00:08:42,936 --> 00:08:45,647
Corner seat says you're wary.
Need to control your environment.

179
00:08:45,856 --> 00:08:48,275
In most people
that creates tension.

180
00:08:48,859 --> 00:08:50,402
But your breathing is relaxed.

181
00:08:52,612 --> 00:08:53,697
What's your trick, Max?

182
00:08:55,532 --> 00:08:57,200
It's... inner peace.

183
00:08:58,201 --> 00:09:00,662
The kind of peace you get
when you shoot your wife's killer?

184
00:09:02,247 --> 00:09:03,790
Henry was innocent it seems.

185
00:09:04,207 --> 00:09:06,835
Well, if that's true the man
that raped and cut your wife up

186
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:08,045
is still out there.

187
00:09:09,755 --> 00:09:11,006
That doesn't bother you?

188
00:09:11,423 --> 00:09:13,008
Jennifer's life ended abruptly.

189
00:09:13,967 --> 00:09:15,927
Could've been a drunk driver
could've been a meteorite.

190
00:09:16,845 --> 00:09:20,682
Anger is unhelpful
and unproductive.

191
00:09:21,266 --> 00:09:22,309
Well, I don't know.

192
00:09:23,185 --> 00:09:26,104
Some people... it's, uh
it's invigorating.

193
00:09:27,939 --> 00:09:30,358
I'm sorry I don't fit your image
of the tragic husband.

194
00:09:33,153 --> 00:09:34,946
You ask where my peace
comes from.

195
00:09:36,990 --> 00:09:39,451
It's called reconciliation.

196
00:09:40,077 --> 00:09:43,288
I can't undo what happened
to my wife

197
00:09:43,455 --> 00:09:45,457
but for the sake
of my son Jackson

198
00:09:46,083 --> 00:09:48,335
I can decide how I wanna
live thereafter.

199
00:09:50,045 --> 00:09:51,379
So I forgive Henry...

200
00:09:51,838 --> 00:09:54,883
Or... whoever it was
who killed Jennie.

201
00:09:56,259 --> 00:09:58,053
I actually forgave them
long ago.

202
00:09:59,638 --> 00:10:01,348
Let's get you wired for sound
Mr. Winter.

203
00:10:02,682 --> 00:10:03,725
Excuse me.

204
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:05,560
- Where's miss Dahl?
- "Right at stage four."

205
00:10:05,685 --> 00:10:07,020
Okay. Bring her to the stage.

206
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:10,941
This was the scene
nine years ago

207
00:10:11,066 --> 00:10:12,776
when Henry Dahl was attacked

208
00:10:12,943 --> 00:10:15,237
before a pretrial hearing
by the husband

209
00:10:15,403 --> 00:10:18,281
of the woman whose throat
he was accused of cutting.

210
00:10:19,491 --> 00:10:20,617
"I'm gonna kill you, Dahl."

211
00:10:21,868 --> 00:10:22,911
"What're you doing?"

212
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:35,423
"Three deputies were guarding
Henry Dahl that day."

213
00:10:35,632 --> 00:10:37,175
"Now that was the Max I knew."

214
00:10:37,342 --> 00:10:40,178
He would be dancing a jig
after hearing Henry Dahl was dead.

215
00:10:40,428 --> 00:10:42,931
But am I to understand
you actually forgave Dahl

216
00:10:43,056 --> 00:10:46,101
before this so-called new
DNA evidence came to light?

217
00:10:46,309 --> 00:10:48,186
"It's called reconciliation, Karen."

218
00:10:49,229 --> 00:10:50,772
- "Reconciliation?"
- "Yeah, reconciliation. In my case... "

219
00:10:50,897 --> 00:10:52,732
"Oh, I'm familiar
with what reconciliation means."

220
00:10:52,899 --> 00:10:55,193
"Reconciliation is basically
when family members of victims"

221
00:10:55,318 --> 00:10:56,361
"they waltz over to the prison"

222
00:10:56,486 --> 00:10:58,280
"they decide they're gonna forgive
this person"

223
00:10:58,405 --> 00:10:59,447
"who murdered
their wife or children."

224
00:10:59,573 --> 00:11:01,074
"I just don't buy it, Max."

225
00:11:07,372 --> 00:11:09,416
{\an8}"He was only here for two nights.
I hardly saw him."

226
00:11:09,541 --> 00:11:12,544
{\an8}"When I did, he was just so happy
to be free. Who wouldn't be?"

227
00:11:12,961 --> 00:11:14,087
Did Mr. Dahl have any visitors?

228
00:11:14,254 --> 00:11:15,422
Yeah, the first night out.

229
00:11:15,547 --> 00:11:17,924
Some woman... she dropped by
for a few hours.

230
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:21,344
Not bad-looking either.
The guy didn't waste much time.

231
00:11:21,678 --> 00:11:24,973
- You catch her name?
- No. Sorry. Here we go.

232
00:11:30,896 --> 00:11:32,480
- Thank you.
- Thanks a lot.

233
00:11:35,942 --> 00:11:37,527
Oh, we can take it from here.
Thank you.

234
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:39,863
You're welcome.

235
00:11:42,449 --> 00:11:43,742
Well, someone was looking
for something.

236
00:11:44,117 --> 00:11:45,744
Thank you
Captain obvious.

237
00:11:46,912 --> 00:11:48,830
Three hours of brooding silence
then sarcasm.

238
00:11:48,997 --> 00:11:50,040
It's like we're married.

239
00:11:50,582 --> 00:11:53,919
Sorry, Cho. It's just special
Agent Craig O'Laughlin

240
00:11:54,044 --> 00:11:55,962
and his... chopper.

241
00:11:56,755 --> 00:11:58,173
What's she see
in that guy anyway?

242
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:00,675
Ivy League degree in criminology

243
00:12:01,259 --> 00:12:03,220
6 years
in the 101st Airborne

244
00:12:04,304 --> 00:12:06,932
and a black belt in Wushu
from a real Shaolin Temple.

245
00:12:07,057 --> 00:12:08,516
Yeah, thanks, buddy.
Whatever.

246
00:12:13,230 --> 00:12:14,272
Cho, under the bed!

247
00:12:24,032 --> 00:12:25,700
- What are you doing?
- Just go! Go!

248
00:12:39,047 --> 00:12:41,132
Bet they don't teach that
at a Shaolin Temple.

249
00:13:00,149 --> 00:13:02,110
"Dad! In the garage!"

250
00:13:08,866 --> 00:13:10,785
Wow. Hi.

251
00:13:11,619 --> 00:13:13,288
I thought you were my dad.

252
00:13:13,663 --> 00:13:14,706
No.

253
00:13:16,916 --> 00:13:17,959
What...

254
00:13:18,543 --> 00:13:20,253
an amazing phenomenon...

255
00:13:21,838 --> 00:13:23,840
flying machines frozen...

256
00:13:24,799 --> 00:13:25,842
In mid-air.

257
00:13:26,759 --> 00:13:29,220
I kinda feel sorry for 'em.
Did... did you do all this?

258
00:13:30,054 --> 00:13:33,391
Um, no, my... my dad does.
I just help out.

259
00:13:34,434 --> 00:13:36,728
You're Max's son.
I-I saw you at the talk show.

260
00:13:37,312 --> 00:13:38,354
Jackson, right?

261
00:13:39,564 --> 00:13:41,524
I-I-I'm... I'm looking
for your dad.

262
00:13:42,483 --> 00:13:43,526
Who are you?

263
00:13:44,193 --> 00:13:47,572
- You... you a cop or something?
- No. Too many laws to uphold.

264
00:13:47,739 --> 00:13:49,949
But I-I do work
with the police.

265
00:13:50,575 --> 00:13:53,745
Don't touch that, please.
It's... it's not cured yet.

266
00:13:54,329 --> 00:13:55,496
It feels okay to me.

267
00:13:55,872 --> 00:13:57,415
It needs two more hours.

268
00:14:02,670 --> 00:14:04,255
Can... can you go now?

269
00:14:06,382 --> 00:14:07,592
Uh, look, my... my dad's
not at the house.

270
00:14:07,717 --> 00:14:08,885
I don't know where he is.

271
00:14:09,761 --> 00:14:10,803
Sure, you do.

272
00:14:11,596 --> 00:14:12,680
You just don't want me to know.

273
00:14:15,516 --> 00:14:16,559
Is he hiding something?

274
00:14:17,977 --> 00:14:19,729
No, he's not hiding anything.

275
00:14:23,066 --> 00:14:25,443
- Are you?
- No. Of course not.

276
00:14:27,278 --> 00:14:29,197
Your dad doesn't seem like
the kind of man

277
00:14:29,322 --> 00:14:31,282
who would ask you to lie.

278
00:14:35,244 --> 00:14:36,287
Artie Mock.

279
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:39,999
"Multiple arrests
for assault, battery"

280
00:14:40,208 --> 00:14:41,250
"battery with injury..."

281
00:14:42,418 --> 00:14:44,379
And today, aggravated assault
against a police officer.

282
00:14:44,504 --> 00:14:45,546
I'm sorry.

283
00:14:46,214 --> 00:14:48,508
- Did I hurt you?
- It was just a mattress.

284
00:14:49,634 --> 00:14:51,177
Now what's your relationship
with Henry Dahl?

285
00:14:52,303 --> 00:14:53,346
Friends.

286
00:14:54,138 --> 00:14:55,473
We met in junior college.

287
00:14:56,933 --> 00:14:59,060
- We were art majors.
- Why'd you break into his room?

288
00:14:59,394 --> 00:15:00,561
I was looking for my money.

289
00:15:01,729 --> 00:15:04,774
He owes me 22 large
plus nine years' interest.

290
00:15:07,026 --> 00:15:08,069
Where were you last night?

291
00:15:08,403 --> 00:15:09,445
Last night?

292
00:15:10,780 --> 00:15:12,198
I was working graveyard
at the plant.

293
00:15:12,323 --> 00:15:13,699
And, no, I didn't kill him.

294
00:15:14,492 --> 00:15:16,411
I mean, now I ain't ever
gonna see what's mine.

295
00:15:16,953 --> 00:15:17,995
What did Dahl owe you for?

296
00:15:19,705 --> 00:15:22,166
Statute of limitations on drug
dealing is what, six years?

297
00:15:22,375 --> 00:15:23,418
Six years.

298
00:15:24,377 --> 00:15:27,505
In that case, Henry and I ran
a small business in college.

299
00:15:29,757 --> 00:15:31,384
We... we weren't very good
at art

300
00:15:31,509 --> 00:15:34,512
so we started
distributing cocaine.

301
00:15:35,263 --> 00:15:37,598
- That's a natural transition.
- That's what we said.

302
00:15:38,266 --> 00:15:39,851
No, H-Henry was storing
a shipment

303
00:15:39,976 --> 00:15:41,519
and... and he gets popped
for murder.

304
00:15:41,769 --> 00:15:43,062
Two kilos.

305
00:15:43,354 --> 00:15:44,856
I visited him in prison
and he tells me

306
00:15:44,981 --> 00:15:47,442
that the... the stash gets
confiscated, so I let it go.

307
00:15:47,859 --> 00:15:49,402
But then the day
that Henry gets outta prison

308
00:15:49,527 --> 00:15:51,863
he starts flossing about taking
his girlfriend to Jamaica

309
00:15:51,988 --> 00:15:55,032
so I'm thinking... he's still got
those drugs hidden.

310
00:15:55,450 --> 00:15:56,576
- His girl?
- Mm-hmm.

311
00:15:57,285 --> 00:15:59,162
Henry'd been out for two days.
When'd he find a girlfriend?

312
00:15:59,495 --> 00:16:01,706
She found him, in prison.

313
00:16:02,790 --> 00:16:03,833
They were pen pals.

314
00:16:05,126 --> 00:16:06,335
According to Del Norte

315
00:16:06,461 --> 00:16:08,754
Henry Dahl had just one name
on his approved e-mail list...

316
00:16:08,963 --> 00:16:11,883
a woman named Betsy Meyers
from Lodi, his hometown.

317
00:16:12,091 --> 00:16:15,845
Lodi... yeah, I was just there today.
Long drive. Hot.

318
00:16:17,263 --> 00:16:19,348
She and Dahl had a thing going
in high school.

319
00:16:19,474 --> 00:16:20,850
She reached out to him
two months ago

320
00:16:21,017 --> 00:16:23,519
rekindled their romance.
But there's just one problem.

321
00:16:23,978 --> 00:16:26,147
- Betsy's married?
- Yes, to a Sean Meyers.

322
00:16:26,314 --> 00:16:27,356
He's a factory worker.

323
00:16:27,482 --> 00:16:28,524
They've been together
for seven years.

324
00:16:28,649 --> 00:16:30,568
- Somebody should question him.
- Agreed.

325
00:16:31,152 --> 00:16:32,653
Someone who hasn't been
to Lodi yet.

326
00:16:32,987 --> 00:16:35,031
I wanna see how willingly
they come to us.

327
00:16:35,239 --> 00:16:36,324
Let's call them in in the morning.

328
00:16:36,491 --> 00:16:38,326
In the meantime,
there's no shortage of suspects

329
00:16:38,451 --> 00:16:39,619
who hated Henry Dahl.

330
00:16:39,869 --> 00:16:42,747
23 people threatened his life
during his murder trial

331
00:16:43,164 --> 00:16:45,833
most of which were friends
or family of the alleged victim.

332
00:16:46,584 --> 00:16:47,668
Here's a list of those names

333
00:16:47,793 --> 00:16:51,672
courtesy of our cable
television friend, Miss Cross.

334
00:16:52,590 --> 00:16:54,467
We got a lotta eyes on us
on this one.

335
00:16:54,634 --> 00:16:56,719
I want you to stay late
and run down these names.

336
00:17:12,527 --> 00:17:13,569
Bravo.

337
00:17:15,321 --> 00:17:16,364
Oh.

338
00:17:21,035 --> 00:17:22,537
I had a feeling
I'd see you again.

339
00:17:23,663 --> 00:17:26,374
Takes a confident man
to visit a shooting range

340
00:17:26,541 --> 00:17:27,792
when he's still
a murder suspect.

341
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,669
Or an innocent one.
I never got your name.

342
00:17:29,794 --> 00:17:31,754
- Sorry?
- Never got your name.

343
00:17:31,879 --> 00:17:34,131
- Uh, Patrick Jane.
- What kinda gun do you use, Patrick?

344
00:17:34,257 --> 00:17:35,675
Oh, no.
I don't... I don't use.

345
00:17:35,967 --> 00:17:39,178
No? That's, uh, a little strange
for a policeman, isn't it?

346
00:17:39,595 --> 00:17:41,639
Me, I-I like
a well-balanced .45...

347
00:17:41,764 --> 00:17:44,600
best combination of power
accuracy, and kick.

348
00:17:46,519 --> 00:17:48,271
- Hey, hey. Careful. Whoa.
- Whoa.

349
00:17:48,479 --> 00:17:50,231
- Whoa, watch the muzzle sweep.
- Sorry.

350
00:17:50,439 --> 00:17:52,066
You never point at anything
you don't wanna shoot.

351
00:18:04,203 --> 00:18:05,246
So...

352
00:18:05,955 --> 00:18:06,998
How can I help you, Patrick?

353
00:18:07,206 --> 00:18:09,709
Well, um, I'm just curious
to hear how you did it.

354
00:18:12,169 --> 00:18:13,212
How I did what?

355
00:18:13,462 --> 00:18:14,755
Forgave your wife's killer.

356
00:18:15,298 --> 00:18:18,551
- Ah. So you believe me now.
- Well, don't get carried away

357
00:18:18,676 --> 00:18:21,304
but I-I did find out
that you've become quite the, uh...

358
00:18:21,637 --> 00:18:25,349
evangelist for reconciliation
corporate talks and all.

359
00:18:25,808 --> 00:18:27,143
You know why I came here
in the first place?

360
00:18:27,268 --> 00:18:28,477
I manage real estate funds.

361
00:18:28,603 --> 00:18:30,062
I never touched a weapon
in my life.

362
00:18:31,981 --> 00:18:33,441
But then Jennie was butchered

363
00:18:34,650 --> 00:18:35,818
and I needed a release.

364
00:18:36,986 --> 00:18:39,030
At first, I pretended
the targets were Henry Dahl

365
00:18:39,614 --> 00:18:42,700
and now, they're just targets.

366
00:18:43,743 --> 00:18:45,286
It's a discipline.
Very Zen.

367
00:18:45,745 --> 00:18:48,205
You found the gift of forgiveness
through firearms.

368
00:18:48,331 --> 00:18:49,999
Yeah, shooting helped me cope.

369
00:18:50,416 --> 00:18:51,792
My son led me to forgiveness.

370
00:18:51,917 --> 00:18:54,920
All this rage inside me
that lust for vengeance...

371
00:18:55,087 --> 00:18:56,922
I could see
I was scaring Jackson.

372
00:18:58,090 --> 00:18:59,383
I didn't wanna lose him too.

373
00:18:59,592 --> 00:19:00,635
Lo and behold

374
00:19:01,010 --> 00:19:03,596
Henry Dahl was exonerated
like a sign from above.

375
00:19:03,721 --> 00:19:06,390
Yes. Exactly. I just wish
I had forgiven him sooner.

376
00:19:07,683 --> 00:19:09,018
Do you have children, Patrick?

377
00:19:10,728 --> 00:19:11,771
No.

378
00:19:20,946 --> 00:19:21,989
Try it.

379
00:19:24,450 --> 00:19:25,534
No better pain relief.

380
00:19:28,954 --> 00:19:30,581
- I'm not in pain.
- No?

381
00:19:32,124 --> 00:19:33,167
I sense pain.

382
00:19:33,584 --> 00:19:35,086
Ah, it's nothing a gun will fix.

383
00:19:36,462 --> 00:19:38,798
By the way
I don't own the gun.

384
00:19:38,923 --> 00:19:40,758
I just rent them
while I'm here.

385
00:19:40,925 --> 00:19:44,553
And I was with my son, at home
at the time of the murder.

386
00:19:44,679 --> 00:19:45,888
Just, uh...

387
00:19:46,972 --> 00:19:48,015
In case you're interested.

388
00:19:49,767 --> 00:19:50,810
It's gonna get loud.

389
00:19:53,521 --> 00:19:54,647
I'll just step back here.

390
00:19:59,944 --> 00:20:01,779
Henry had a beautiful spirit.

391
00:20:02,613 --> 00:20:05,783
That whole bad boy thing...
that was always just an act.

392
00:20:07,368 --> 00:20:09,495
He even wanted to try acting
when he got outta prison.

393
00:20:10,162 --> 00:20:11,205
How did you know that?

394
00:20:11,497 --> 00:20:13,666
He told me...
in his e-mails.

395
00:20:15,876 --> 00:20:18,045
Did you know that your wife
was exchanging e-mails

396
00:20:18,170 --> 00:20:19,255
with a convicted felon?

397
00:20:19,547 --> 00:20:21,590
Yeah, I was taking dictation for her.

398
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:23,843
What do you think? No.

399
00:20:24,510 --> 00:20:25,553
Betsy, according to the prison

400
00:20:25,678 --> 00:20:27,471
you'd been writing Henry
for two months.

401
00:20:27,972 --> 00:20:30,224
- Why'd you reach out to him?
- Yeah, why did you?

402
00:20:32,017 --> 00:20:33,352
You've had a first love, right?

403
00:20:33,936 --> 00:20:35,062
"You know that feeling?"

404
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:38,941
"It never really goes away
not totally."

405
00:20:40,985 --> 00:20:42,778
I never thought
that Henry killed anyone

406
00:20:42,903 --> 00:20:44,989
and when I read that there was
new DNA evidence

407
00:20:45,156 --> 00:20:48,159
I just wanted to say
"Woot, woot," you know

408
00:20:48,743 --> 00:20:49,785
as a friend.

409
00:20:51,370 --> 00:20:52,705
Does this sound like friends?

410
00:20:53,789 --> 00:20:56,500
"Dear Henry
wild thoughts in my head."

411
00:20:56,667 --> 00:20:59,295
"I know I shouldn't wonder
what your body feels like now"

412
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:01,464
- "but it's all I... "
- Okay, stop. All right?

413
00:21:02,506 --> 00:21:03,966
Why are you doing this to me?

414
00:21:04,467 --> 00:21:06,552
My husband is sitting right here.

415
00:21:08,763 --> 00:21:09,805
I'm sorry.

416
00:21:10,765 --> 00:21:11,807
"I'm so sorry."

417
00:21:12,266 --> 00:21:14,059
She is so full of it.

418
00:21:14,894 --> 00:21:17,855
What? She wrote some stuff to a guy.
There's worse crimes.

419
00:21:18,272 --> 00:21:19,523
"Betsy, tell me the truth."

420
00:21:19,690 --> 00:21:22,693
- You heard. She's in love with him.
- No, that's not true!

421
00:21:25,196 --> 00:21:27,448
I really just started
writing him as a friend.

422
00:21:29,450 --> 00:21:31,202
We were arguing so much.

423
00:21:32,244 --> 00:21:33,662
And he knew me so well.

424
00:21:34,705 --> 00:21:36,207
"He started saying
all the things to me"

425
00:21:36,332 --> 00:21:38,000
"that you stopped saying
years ago."

426
00:21:41,337 --> 00:21:43,506
"Did you have any contact
with him after he was released?"

427
00:21:43,672 --> 00:21:45,466
"No. Only in e-mails."

428
00:21:46,383 --> 00:21:49,011
Betsy, we showed a photo
of you to the manager

429
00:21:49,136 --> 00:21:50,971
of the halfway house
Henry was living in.

430
00:21:51,347 --> 00:21:53,516
"He identified you as the woman
who visited Henry"

431
00:21:53,641 --> 00:21:55,100
"the day after his release."

432
00:21:57,770 --> 00:21:58,813
"You slept with him?"

433
00:22:00,606 --> 00:22:02,900
- Oh, hush.
- I didn't say anything.

434
00:22:04,318 --> 00:22:05,569
What is this, marriage counsel?

435
00:22:05,945 --> 00:22:07,238
They're suspects
in the shooting.

436
00:22:07,947 --> 00:22:08,989
These two?

437
00:22:09,240 --> 00:22:10,825
Really? Hmm.

438
00:22:12,076 --> 00:22:13,118
Um...

439
00:22:13,994 --> 00:22:15,746
- Can I see your gun?
- Hmm?

440
00:22:16,914 --> 00:22:18,415
Where were the 2 of you
at 1:00 A.M.?

441
00:22:18,791 --> 00:22:20,376
Drinking beers at the Lamplighter.

442
00:22:21,418 --> 00:22:24,129
Hi. Patrick Jane.
Very nice to meet you.

443
00:22:24,255 --> 00:22:25,297
- Hold that, please.
- Whoa.

444
00:22:25,422 --> 00:22:26,465
- What?
- I'm sorry. Here.

445
00:22:26,590 --> 00:22:29,426
- What the hell are you doing?
- It's okay. Rigsby took the bullets out.

446
00:22:29,593 --> 00:22:32,805
Muzzle sweeps. They have
no idea how to handle a gun.

447
00:22:32,930 --> 00:22:33,973
Give me a break.

448
00:22:34,098 --> 00:22:37,309
"Van Pelt, run down
Betsy and Sean's alibis."

449
00:22:37,434 --> 00:22:38,644
You, outside!

450
00:22:39,395 --> 00:22:40,437
Excuse me.

451
00:22:43,315 --> 00:22:45,109
So you're a gun expert now?

452
00:22:45,234 --> 00:22:47,695
No, not me. Max Winter.
You should see him shoot.

453
00:22:47,820 --> 00:22:50,030
- Did he shoot Dahl?
- What caliber was the murder weapon?

454
00:22:50,322 --> 00:22:52,366
- 9 millimeter.
- Yeah, Max prefers a .45.

455
00:22:52,533 --> 00:22:54,577
Oh, shucks.
I guess he's innocent.

456
00:22:55,035 --> 00:22:57,413
Here's a revised suspect list.
Max is still on it.

457
00:22:57,663 --> 00:22:59,290
And I hope you got
some sleep last night

458
00:22:59,415 --> 00:23:01,792
because we're going to Lodi
to run interviews.

459
00:23:02,167 --> 00:23:03,502
I just got back from Lodi.

460
00:23:03,752 --> 00:23:05,754
Van leaves in 20 minutes.
Be outside.

461
00:23:11,969 --> 00:23:14,054
Patrick Jane.
I didn't recognize you yesterday

462
00:23:14,179 --> 00:23:15,222
at the crime scene.

463
00:23:15,431 --> 00:23:16,765
You were quite the psychic once.

464
00:23:16,932 --> 00:23:18,434
Oh, there's no such thing
as psychics.

465
00:23:18,684 --> 00:23:19,727
So you say now.

466
00:23:21,061 --> 00:23:23,355
Your cold reading of a serial killer
on live television

467
00:23:23,480 --> 00:23:25,482
led to the brutal murder
of your wife and daughter.

468
00:23:25,816 --> 00:23:28,027
Oh. Really?
Thanks for reminding me.

469
00:23:28,402 --> 00:23:30,404
Mr. Jane, I am on your side.

470
00:23:30,529 --> 00:23:31,947
You were a victim
of violent crime

471
00:23:32,072 --> 00:23:34,074
and now you help others.
That's wonderful.

472
00:23:34,575 --> 00:23:36,660
Don't tell me you don't have
the psychic stuff.

473
00:23:36,994 --> 00:23:39,121
What are you doing for the CBI
if you're not psychic?

474
00:23:39,747 --> 00:23:41,248
Are you scamming them
Mr. Jane?

475
00:23:41,415 --> 00:23:42,958
No, Karen, I am not.

476
00:23:43,125 --> 00:23:45,878
Well, prove it.
Identify Dahl's killer.

477
00:23:48,213 --> 00:23:50,716
Well, if I had carte blanche
to do things my way

478
00:23:50,841 --> 00:23:53,052
I would do just that, but they don't
give me carte blanche.

479
00:23:53,218 --> 00:23:54,803
The CBI won't, but I will.

480
00:23:55,638 --> 00:23:56,680
Deputy?

481
00:23:58,182 --> 00:23:59,224
You've been served.

482
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:03,062
You're hereby commanded to testify
before a studio audience of your peers.

483
00:24:03,896 --> 00:24:05,272
What do you need
to solve this crime?

484
00:24:05,564 --> 00:24:06,607
Name it.

485
00:24:08,233 --> 00:24:10,069
Well, I suppose, uh...

486
00:24:10,194 --> 00:24:13,030
I need the help of those
that are affected by Dahl's murder.

487
00:24:13,405 --> 00:24:16,033
Those two you had on your show
yesterday would do.

488
00:24:16,575 --> 00:24:17,618
I'll bring them back.

489
00:24:18,494 --> 00:24:21,497
And, uh, I guess
uh, I need suspects.

490
00:24:22,039 --> 00:24:24,792
Yeah. Make sure these people
are in the audience.

491
00:24:25,125 --> 00:24:27,002
Give 'em an appearance fee
whatever it takes.

492
00:24:39,014 --> 00:24:40,349
Beats driving back to Lodi.

493
00:24:50,732 --> 00:24:54,903
{\an8}"You saw him here first...
the sleeping CBI Agent."

494
00:24:55,195 --> 00:24:57,698
{\an8}"Well, get ready to meet him."

495
00:24:57,990 --> 00:25:01,660
{\an8}"Patrick Jane... psychic detective?
Tragic victim?"

496
00:25:01,952 --> 00:25:03,328
{\an8}"You be the judge today at 3:00"

497
00:25:03,453 --> 00:25:05,747
{\an8}"when Mr. Jane taps
his mind's eye"

498
00:25:05,872 --> 00:25:07,958
{\an8}"to reveal who killed Henry Dahl."

499
00:25:08,083 --> 00:25:09,126
This is good?

500
00:25:10,585 --> 00:25:12,129
Well, where is he?

501
00:25:13,130 --> 00:25:14,172
We're five minutes to air.

502
00:25:14,297 --> 00:25:15,924
Without him, it's just
a rehash of yesterday's show.

503
00:25:16,049 --> 00:25:17,175
I've left messages everywhere.

504
00:25:18,301 --> 00:25:19,344
Oh.

505
00:25:19,803 --> 00:25:23,056
Oh. Sorry I'm late. I had to stop
by a Bed and Bath Boutique.

506
00:25:23,432 --> 00:25:25,392
Mr. Jane, there is a reason
that I asked you

507
00:25:25,517 --> 00:25:26,560
to arrive two hours early.

508
00:25:26,685 --> 00:25:28,020
I need to walk you through
the lineup.

509
00:25:28,145 --> 00:25:30,397
- Are all my suspects here?
- Yeah, they're in the first two rows.

510
00:25:30,814 --> 00:25:33,066
Now the lineup... I'll start
with my state of the union

511
00:25:33,358 --> 00:25:34,484
and then we're just gonna roll a clip

512
00:25:34,609 --> 00:25:35,652
- to introduce you.
- A clip?

513
00:25:35,777 --> 00:25:38,113
Uh, y... well
just some old footage.

514
00:25:38,989 --> 00:25:40,032
- My Red John talk show appearance.
- Oh, no.

515
00:25:40,157 --> 00:25:42,325
Just save... save
your reaction for on-air.

516
00:25:42,451 --> 00:25:43,493
I really want you
to relive the moment...

517
00:25:43,618 --> 00:25:45,412
Excuse me, Karen.
I gotta hand out candles.

518
00:25:45,954 --> 00:25:47,330
Candles? Wait.
What... what candles?

519
00:25:47,998 --> 00:25:49,041
Hey, folks.

520
00:25:49,458 --> 00:25:53,170
I hope Lodi is treating you well.
A little treat from Karen Cross.

521
00:25:53,712 --> 00:25:56,131
Hand them out. One each.

522
00:25:56,631 --> 00:25:58,800
Here you go. Pass that back
to him, please. Thank you.

523
00:25:59,509 --> 00:26:01,011
Ah, Jackson.

524
00:26:02,304 --> 00:26:04,181
Here to see the old man again
are you? Here.

525
00:26:05,807 --> 00:26:06,850
Have a candle.

526
00:26:07,517 --> 00:26:08,977
We need you onstage now.

527
00:26:09,102 --> 00:26:10,896
Oh, I have to hand these out.
They're for my act.

528
00:26:11,354 --> 00:26:12,856
Unless, uh, what are you doing?

529
00:26:14,608 --> 00:26:16,234
Everybody must have one.

530
00:26:17,110 --> 00:26:18,820
Okay?
I need these. Thank you.

531
00:26:22,032 --> 00:26:23,241
- Patrick.
- Max.

532
00:26:38,924 --> 00:26:40,717
You've probably read stories

533
00:26:40,842 --> 00:26:44,346
about psychics who help
the police solve murder cases.

534
00:26:44,638 --> 00:26:48,266
Well, today, we're gonna take
a peek behind the mystic's veil

535
00:26:48,433 --> 00:26:50,018
to see how that's done.

536
00:26:50,352 --> 00:26:52,479
To introduce our featured guest

537
00:26:52,854 --> 00:26:55,315
we have a dramatic video clip
of his last talk show.

538
00:26:55,440 --> 00:26:57,275
Uh, Karen, sorry to interrupt.

539
00:26:58,026 --> 00:26:59,569
Uh, Mr. Jane, I'll get to you
in a minute.

540
00:26:59,694 --> 00:27:00,737
Could you please sit down?

541
00:27:00,862 --> 00:27:03,657
You... you... you wanted to tell me
something about the lineup.

542
00:27:03,824 --> 00:27:06,743
I forget. The lineup...
did you tell me?

543
00:27:07,452 --> 00:27:09,830
The lineup, I forget.
Forget the lineup.

544
00:27:10,956 --> 00:27:11,998
What is the lineup?

545
00:27:12,999 --> 00:27:14,209
- Uh... I'm...
- Let me remind you.

546
00:27:14,334 --> 00:27:17,420
Ladies and gentlemen
it is time to unmask the shooter.

547
00:27:18,880 --> 00:27:20,257
What you have all
just witnessed

548
00:27:20,382 --> 00:27:23,718
is a remarkable piece
of mental tomfoolery

549
00:27:23,927 --> 00:27:26,763
"from our psychic guest star
Patrick Jane."

550
00:27:26,930 --> 00:27:29,933
However, this is not
Mr. Jane's show

551
00:27:30,100 --> 00:27:32,853
so I'm gonna ask Mr. Jane again
to please sit down.

552
00:27:33,019 --> 00:27:36,106
I'm sorry. I forget. Whose show is it?
Whose show? I forget.

553
00:27:36,773 --> 00:27:37,941
I forget whose show.
Forget...

554
00:27:38,066 --> 00:27:40,777
W-will you stop doing that?
You are a guest here.

555
00:27:40,944 --> 00:27:42,612
Well, actually, no.
I was, uh, summoned.

556
00:27:44,281 --> 00:27:45,907
Are you sure this is
how you wanna play it?

557
00:27:46,158 --> 00:27:49,035
You really wanna make yourself
a sorrier spectacle?

558
00:27:49,202 --> 00:27:50,996
Oh, is there anyone more sorry
than you, Karen

559
00:27:51,121 --> 00:27:54,207
with your morbid interest in all things
shocking or repulsive?

560
00:27:54,875 --> 00:27:57,627
You pretend to be a victim's advocate
but you're a vulture

561
00:27:57,794 --> 00:28:00,046
preying on the carrion
of human suffering.

562
00:28:00,172 --> 00:28:01,214
Are you gonna give me
that carte blanche

563
00:28:01,339 --> 00:28:03,133
- you promised me or not?
- Yeah, buddy, go.

564
00:28:03,383 --> 00:28:04,426
Yeah.

565
00:28:05,302 --> 00:28:06,887
You gonna give me
that carte blanche?

566
00:28:09,181 --> 00:28:11,433
"I feel like getting me
some carte blanche."

567
00:28:11,933 --> 00:28:15,812
Okay, Mr. Jane
let the psychic wonders unfurl.

568
00:28:17,981 --> 00:28:20,483
There's no such thing
as psychics, Karen.

569
00:28:21,067 --> 00:28:24,112
Only entertainers skilled
in the art of deception.

570
00:28:24,863 --> 00:28:28,909
But I'm happy to, uh, perform
a psychic "demonstration".

571
00:28:30,327 --> 00:28:33,371
Usually begins
with a fantastic claim

572
00:28:33,496 --> 00:28:38,418
such as Henry Dahl's killer
is sitting in this room right now.

573
00:28:39,961 --> 00:28:42,505
And then, uh, any good psychic

574
00:28:42,756 --> 00:28:44,049
would make sure
that the front row

575
00:28:44,174 --> 00:28:46,468
is full of actual police suspects

576
00:28:46,593 --> 00:28:48,678
which I believe that we have done
for you today.

577
00:28:49,679 --> 00:28:51,389
Then he might go on and explain

578
00:28:51,556 --> 00:28:54,309
how, uh, a-a spirit
that has crossed over

579
00:28:54,601 --> 00:28:58,313
still has the power to manipulate
energy in our world...

580
00:28:58,980 --> 00:29:01,316
creating noises, moving objects

581
00:29:01,441 --> 00:29:03,818
and sometimes
even starting fires...

582
00:29:04,027 --> 00:29:07,322
and that is what Henry Dahl
is gonna do for us right now.

583
00:29:08,198 --> 00:29:10,492
Henry Dahl will light a candle

584
00:29:11,368 --> 00:29:13,954
to reveal his shooter.

585
00:29:16,331 --> 00:29:19,251
- Dim the lights, please.
- Oh, I can't wait for this.

586
00:29:19,459 --> 00:29:20,961
That's the spirit.
That's your candle.

587
00:29:21,127 --> 00:29:22,420
Oh. There is one other thing.

588
00:29:22,545 --> 00:29:25,507
A truly world-class psychic
would invite police officials

589
00:29:25,632 --> 00:29:27,884
up on the stage
to make an arrest

590
00:29:28,260 --> 00:29:30,428
once the killer
has been unmasked.

591
00:29:30,595 --> 00:29:31,888
And today
we are very fortunate

592
00:29:32,013 --> 00:29:35,433
to have some, uh, conveniently
telegenic CBI officers...

593
00:29:35,558 --> 00:29:37,352
uh, Agent Wayne Rigsby

594
00:29:37,602 --> 00:29:41,064
and the pocket rocket
the dynamite, heavily armed

595
00:29:41,189 --> 00:29:43,483
cute as a button
Agent Teresa Lisbon.

596
00:29:43,608 --> 00:29:45,694
Ladies and gentlemen
a round of applause.

597
00:29:50,073 --> 00:29:52,117
Ladies and gentlemen, please

598
00:29:52,993 --> 00:29:55,078
raise your candles
high above your heads

599
00:29:55,287 --> 00:29:59,165
while we wait for Henry Dahl
to reveal his shooter.

600
00:30:02,711 --> 00:30:04,045
"Speak to us, Henry."

601
00:30:05,297 --> 00:30:07,090
"Speak to us from beyond"

602
00:30:07,507 --> 00:30:08,758
the great beyond.

603
00:30:20,562 --> 00:30:22,063
This is ridiculous...

604
00:30:34,075 --> 00:30:36,119
- Wait.
- Jackson Winter.

605
00:30:37,203 --> 00:30:38,246
Of course.

606
00:30:39,247 --> 00:30:40,457
Uh, you think it was me?

607
00:30:40,665 --> 00:30:42,667
Henry Dahl was convicted
of killing your mother

608
00:30:42,792 --> 00:30:46,171
but you didn't buy the DNA test
that set him free, did you?

609
00:30:46,629 --> 00:30:49,424
- What are you doing?
- Of course he didn't believe the DNA

610
00:30:49,549 --> 00:30:52,218
I presented an ironclad case.

611
00:30:52,469 --> 00:30:55,347
And the owner of a certain
firing range I visited

612
00:30:55,597 --> 00:30:58,683
tells me that Jackson, in fact
does like to shoot guns.

613
00:30:59,225 --> 00:31:00,769
Prefers a 9 millimeter

614
00:31:01,269 --> 00:31:03,063
same caliber
as the murder weapon.

615
00:31:03,188 --> 00:31:05,857
Look... I didn't shoot anyone, okay?
Dad...

616
00:31:05,982 --> 00:31:10,153
What's more, forensics matched
red fibers from the murder scene

617
00:31:10,570 --> 00:31:12,238
with carpet fibers
from the firing range.

618
00:31:12,405 --> 00:31:14,324
Stop it. You stop this.

619
00:31:14,657 --> 00:31:15,867
My son didn't do anything.

620
00:31:16,242 --> 00:31:18,828
Casting blame on him
like this... it'll ruin his life.

621
00:31:18,953 --> 00:31:19,996
I know.

622
00:31:20,747 --> 00:31:23,166
- Then don't do it.
- Don't let me.

623
00:31:25,668 --> 00:31:27,420
All right.
You leave him alone.

624
00:31:28,671 --> 00:31:29,714
It was me.

625
00:31:30,340 --> 00:31:31,508
I shot Henry Dahl.

626
00:31:34,719 --> 00:31:37,097
Or he is just lying
to protect his son.

627
00:31:37,222 --> 00:31:39,307
- Agents, arrest him.
- I'm not lying.

628
00:31:39,474 --> 00:31:41,226
He's not lying.
His son told me.

629
00:31:44,270 --> 00:31:46,106
What are you talking about?
No, I didn't.

630
00:31:46,314 --> 00:31:48,483
Epoxy takes 12 hours to cure.

631
00:31:49,776 --> 00:31:51,403
Yesterday you told me that
that model

632
00:31:51,528 --> 00:31:52,779
only needed two more hours.

633
00:31:52,946 --> 00:31:55,031
That puts you working in the garage
in the middle of the night.

634
00:31:55,156 --> 00:31:56,241
Your son wasn't asleep.

635
00:31:56,366 --> 00:31:59,452
He heard you leaving. That's why
he wanted to protect you, right?

636
00:31:59,619 --> 00:32:00,662
All right, let him be.

637
00:32:05,542 --> 00:32:08,962
I pulled the trigger
and I can prove it.

638
00:32:20,346 --> 00:32:21,722
You'll find his prints
on the gun.

639
00:32:22,598 --> 00:32:24,267
Ballistics will match the casings.

640
00:32:24,725 --> 00:32:25,810
How did you obtain it?

641
00:32:26,227 --> 00:32:27,728
Gun trafficker, eight years ago.

642
00:32:28,271 --> 00:32:30,565
You purchased an illegal weapon
eight years ago. Why?

643
00:32:32,483 --> 00:32:33,901
Dahl was a lifer
in a state pen.

644
00:32:34,026 --> 00:32:35,528
You had no way of knowing
he'd get out.

645
00:32:40,032 --> 00:32:41,742
Oh, you wanna stop talking now?
That's fine.

646
00:32:42,243 --> 00:32:43,286
All bets are off.

647
00:32:44,412 --> 00:32:45,746
We're gonna take you
and your son into custody.

648
00:32:45,872 --> 00:32:47,123
No, j-just...

649
00:32:48,207 --> 00:32:49,250
Just wait.

650
00:32:52,628 --> 00:32:54,589
I knew Henry was gonna get out

651
00:32:55,381 --> 00:32:57,258
"because I'm the one
who set him free."

652
00:32:59,135 --> 00:33:00,178
How'd you know?

653
00:33:00,428 --> 00:33:03,222
Well, he's a man
of infinite patience

654
00:33:03,389 --> 00:33:04,807
and suppressed rage.

655
00:33:05,641 --> 00:33:07,852
His wife's killer had to die
by his own hand.

656
00:33:08,811 --> 00:33:09,854
He had no choice.

657
00:33:11,564 --> 00:33:13,107
"Okay, you're gonna have
to help me out a little bit."

658
00:33:13,483 --> 00:33:14,817
How did you set Dahl free?

659
00:33:15,067 --> 00:33:17,361
I'm a fund manager
at a real estate investment firm.

660
00:33:17,945 --> 00:33:20,573
I used one fund
to buy up old warehouses...

661
00:33:20,865 --> 00:33:22,783
safe investment
convert 'em into condos...

662
00:33:22,950 --> 00:33:26,287
and that's how my firm came to own
a certain Bay area warehouse

663
00:33:26,579 --> 00:33:27,997
used for evidence storage.

664
00:33:28,331 --> 00:33:29,415
Including DNA.

665
00:33:29,665 --> 00:33:31,626
"It took since months
to pull the condo permits."

666
00:33:31,792 --> 00:33:33,836
"I used that time to gain access
to the property"

667
00:33:33,961 --> 00:33:36,005
and I swapped out
Dahl's DNA sample.

668
00:33:36,380 --> 00:33:39,008
Dahl passes retest.
You kill Dahl.

669
00:33:40,134 --> 00:33:41,552
- You should sell the book rights.
- Hold on.

670
00:33:41,677 --> 00:33:43,262
My client never said
he killed Henry Dahl.

671
00:33:43,387 --> 00:33:45,056
Your client confessed
on basic cable.

672
00:33:45,598 --> 00:33:47,475
To shooting Dahl.

673
00:33:47,934 --> 00:33:48,976
Hello.

674
00:33:49,268 --> 00:33:50,853
I shot that piece of filth.

675
00:33:51,312 --> 00:33:53,231
I shot him right in the face.

676
00:33:55,566 --> 00:33:57,777
But I didn't kill Henry Dahl.

677
00:33:59,028 --> 00:34:00,112
He was already dead.

678
00:34:02,073 --> 00:34:03,115
That's a development.

679
00:34:03,658 --> 00:34:05,034
How do you know Dahl
was dead?

680
00:34:05,868 --> 00:34:07,537
Back of his head
was busted open.

681
00:34:07,662 --> 00:34:09,163
He was lying
in a pool of blood.

682
00:34:09,288 --> 00:34:10,873
- Maybe he was still breathing.
- No.

683
00:34:11,499 --> 00:34:12,875
I checked for a pulse.

684
00:34:13,918 --> 00:34:14,961
I don't buy it.

685
00:34:15,461 --> 00:34:18,714
If you knew Dahl was dead
why'd you shoot him nine times?

686
00:34:26,764 --> 00:34:28,391
Because he murdered my wife.

687
00:34:29,934 --> 00:34:30,977
Uh-oh.

688
00:34:31,894 --> 00:34:32,937
What?

689
00:34:36,691 --> 00:34:37,942
He's telling the truth.

690
00:34:39,610 --> 00:34:41,112
With no head left to speak of

691
00:34:41,237 --> 00:34:42,738
the coroner can't make
a definitely call

692
00:34:42,905 --> 00:34:45,074
but he did say there was less
blood loss than expected

693
00:34:45,199 --> 00:34:47,243
which supports
postmortem gunshots.

694
00:34:47,535 --> 00:34:48,578
Oh, in that case...

695
00:34:49,704 --> 00:34:51,414
Let's go pick up
the actual killer, shall we?

696
00:34:52,999 --> 00:34:54,542
It's kind of obvious now, isn't it?

697
00:34:55,084 --> 00:34:57,503
I mean, shooting is a crime
of planning. That's Max.

698
00:34:57,878 --> 00:35:01,507
He built an entire fleet of planes
with his hands.

699
00:35:02,341 --> 00:35:04,427
- But a blow to the head?
- Crime of passion.

700
00:35:05,177 --> 00:35:07,680
A lot of emotions were flying around
that interrogation room yesterday.

701
00:35:08,014 --> 00:35:10,141
The jilted husband.
Sean Meyers... I felt his pain.

702
00:35:10,433 --> 00:35:12,018
Meyers said he didn't know

703
00:35:12,143 --> 00:35:13,686
his wife was having
an affair with the victim.

704
00:35:13,811 --> 00:35:14,854
Maybe he was lying.

705
00:35:15,813 --> 00:35:16,856
What about his alibi?

706
00:35:16,981 --> 00:35:18,190
He alibi'ed out
for the 1:00 A.M. shooting

707
00:35:18,316 --> 00:35:19,942
but a blow to the head
would've occurred earlier.

708
00:35:20,610 --> 00:35:22,820
It's like watching a symphony orchestra
come together.

709
00:35:27,950 --> 00:35:29,035
Dead River bridge.

710
00:35:32,705 --> 00:35:34,081
Sean Meyers' cell phone server

711
00:35:34,206 --> 00:35:36,125
got a fix on his signal
somewhere around here.

712
00:35:36,459 --> 00:35:37,501
Why would he come out here?

713
00:35:37,627 --> 00:35:39,128
Van Pelt talked
to the apartment manager.

714
00:35:39,253 --> 00:35:40,421
He heard a couple fighting.

715
00:35:40,588 --> 00:35:42,757
Betsy got in her car.
Sean took off after her.

716
00:35:42,923 --> 00:35:44,091
She could be his next victim.

717
00:35:45,468 --> 00:35:46,594
Well, that's one theory.

718
00:35:47,219 --> 00:35:48,262
"Get back!"

719
00:35:48,596 --> 00:35:50,723
- "Betsy!"
- "Stay away from me!"

720
00:35:51,015 --> 00:35:52,725
- "Get over here!"
- Okay.

721
00:35:58,022 --> 00:35:59,815
We're with the CBI.
What's going on here?

722
00:36:00,358 --> 00:36:01,859
Oh, they were up there
when we arrived.

723
00:36:02,652 --> 00:36:03,694
Can't tell yet

724
00:36:03,861 --> 00:36:05,321
but it looks like
an attempted assault.

725
00:36:06,822 --> 00:36:07,865
He's gonna reach her soon.

726
00:36:08,324 --> 00:36:10,451
- "Betsy!"
- We may have to shoot to stop him.

727
00:36:12,620 --> 00:36:13,663
Get back!

728
00:36:13,788 --> 00:36:14,955
Stay away from me!

729
00:36:21,295 --> 00:36:22,338
Betsy!

730
00:36:22,463 --> 00:36:24,131
If you shoot him
you'll have two victims.

731
00:36:24,256 --> 00:36:26,050
She just went up there
to see about jumping.

732
00:36:26,175 --> 00:36:27,301
He's trying to save her.

733
00:36:29,095 --> 00:36:30,137
Can I use that?

734
00:36:35,309 --> 00:36:36,519
Uh, Betsy?

735
00:36:39,271 --> 00:36:42,900
If you're gonna jump
make sure to dive, headfirst.

736
00:36:43,109 --> 00:36:44,151
- Hey.
- Jane.

737
00:36:44,485 --> 00:36:45,945
From that height
you'll just shatter bones

738
00:36:46,070 --> 00:36:47,905
and suffer
massive internal injuries.

739
00:36:48,072 --> 00:36:50,700
Make sure you dive, headfirst.

740
00:36:55,413 --> 00:36:57,331
I just want this all
to end now!

741
00:36:57,915 --> 00:36:58,958
I know.

742
00:36:59,750 --> 00:37:01,252
What do I have to live for?

743
00:37:01,752 --> 00:37:04,714
Um, nothing.
Your husband killed Henry Dahl.

744
00:37:05,047 --> 00:37:06,090
He's going to prison.

745
00:37:10,219 --> 00:37:11,345
You killed Henry?

746
00:37:12,638 --> 00:37:13,723
He was gonna hurt you.

747
00:37:14,640 --> 00:37:15,850
You did that for me, baby?

748
00:37:25,693 --> 00:37:26,736
I got you.

749
00:37:28,279 --> 00:37:30,030
Ah, ain't love grand?

750
00:37:31,198 --> 00:37:32,241
Thank you.

751
00:37:34,076 --> 00:37:35,786
Yeah, she was never
gonna jump anyway.

752
00:37:36,495 --> 00:37:37,830
She just wanted the attention.

753
00:37:43,335 --> 00:37:45,880
I found the e-mails
on her laptop two weeks ago.

754
00:37:47,131 --> 00:37:48,799
My entire world fell apart.

755
00:37:50,468 --> 00:37:52,303
I couldn't lose Betsy.
I just couldn't.

756
00:37:54,388 --> 00:37:56,390
When Henry got out of prison
I went to the motel

757
00:37:56,515 --> 00:37:58,017
but I went to talk with him.

758
00:37:59,310 --> 00:38:00,436
"I'm a free man now."

759
00:38:03,063 --> 00:38:04,440
"Means free love, too."

760
00:38:05,775 --> 00:38:08,110
- "She's married."
- "I'll share her with you."

761
00:38:09,570 --> 00:38:10,613
"I don't mind."

762
00:38:11,489 --> 00:38:13,908
"You learn to share...
in lockdown."

763
00:38:15,075 --> 00:38:17,036
"Where are my damn cigarettes?"

764
00:38:17,453 --> 00:38:18,496
"I'll pay you."

765
00:38:19,413 --> 00:38:21,207
"I'll give you money
to stay away from Betsy."

766
00:38:21,415 --> 00:38:22,458
"How much?"

767
00:38:23,167 --> 00:38:24,543
"I've got 3,200 saved."

768
00:38:26,670 --> 00:38:27,963
"I've got millions coming my way"

769
00:38:28,088 --> 00:38:30,424
"once the state reimburses me
for all that time in jail."

770
00:38:31,926 --> 00:38:32,968
"Sorry."

771
00:38:34,845 --> 00:38:36,972
"I plan on having a little fun
with your wife."

772
00:38:38,933 --> 00:38:39,975
"Maybe I'll..."

773
00:38:40,893 --> 00:38:41,936
"slash her throat..."

774
00:38:43,187 --> 00:38:44,355
"like I did the last one."

775
00:38:48,108 --> 00:38:49,485
"Damn, where are those cigarettes?"

776
00:38:58,702 --> 00:39:00,079
I just wanted to protect her.

777
00:39:00,913 --> 00:39:03,582
This is all my fault.
I'm so sorry.

778
00:39:06,585 --> 00:39:07,711
All right, time to go.

779
00:39:13,634 --> 00:39:14,760
I'll write you every day.

780
00:39:19,723 --> 00:39:21,100
I suppose from your perspective

781
00:39:21,225 --> 00:39:23,936
it's a shame that neither man
will serve any real time.

782
00:39:24,103 --> 00:39:25,145
Oh, will they not?

783
00:39:25,271 --> 00:39:27,940
Well, who knows who really
killed Henry Dahl?

784
00:39:28,148 --> 00:39:30,317
Well, prosecution
will say Sean Meyers.

785
00:39:30,484 --> 00:39:32,903
- He struck him in the head.
- Ah, but did he kill him?

786
00:39:33,404 --> 00:39:34,947
Max may well have lied
to protect himself.

787
00:39:35,072 --> 00:39:36,824
Maybe Dahl was still alive.

788
00:39:37,575 --> 00:39:39,493
Max's attorneys point the finger at Sean

789
00:39:39,618 --> 00:39:41,787
and Sean's attorneys point at Max

790
00:39:41,954 --> 00:39:43,372
and in the end, they both walk.

791
00:39:43,998 --> 00:39:45,291
Yep, that could happen.

792
00:39:45,624 --> 00:39:48,043
Ah, maybe we could save everyone
a lotta time and money

793
00:39:48,168 --> 00:39:49,670
and let 'em both walk now.

794
00:39:49,795 --> 00:39:51,755
After all, the killer did get
what he deserved.

795
00:39:52,464 --> 00:39:53,507
That's common sense.

796
00:39:55,384 --> 00:39:58,804
But, uh, common sense
carries very little weight

797
00:39:58,929 --> 00:40:00,097
in the DA's office.

798
00:40:01,682 --> 00:40:02,725
Pity.

799
00:40:08,856 --> 00:40:10,733
- What you doing there, Rigsby?
- Nothing.

800
00:40:11,191 --> 00:40:12,693
He's trying to light the candle
with the power of his mind.

801
00:40:12,818 --> 00:40:13,861
No, I wasn't.

802
00:40:13,986 --> 00:40:15,654
Is that one of those candles
Jane used on the talk show?

803
00:40:16,280 --> 00:40:17,823
- Look, I know how he did that.
- Oh, yeah?

804
00:40:18,407 --> 00:40:20,200
A candle wick dipped
in liquid phosphorous

805
00:40:20,326 --> 00:40:22,578
will spontaneously
burst into flame when it dries.

806
00:40:22,745 --> 00:40:24,496
The oxygen hits the phosphorous.

807
00:40:24,622 --> 00:40:25,873
Jane must've dipped
the kid's candle in the stuff

808
00:40:25,998 --> 00:40:28,125
before he gave it to him.
Now the real trick, though

809
00:40:28,500 --> 00:40:30,586
is in the timing
which Jane nailed.

810
00:40:30,711 --> 00:40:32,546
Hey. Where are we going
for lunch today?

811
00:40:32,838 --> 00:40:34,214
- Napa.
- Nice.

812
00:40:35,090 --> 00:40:36,133
Bye, guys.

813
00:40:47,269 --> 00:40:49,813
Uh, excuse me, Patrick.
I was looking for you.

814
00:40:50,439 --> 00:40:52,524
It seems they're gonna drop
the attempted murder charge.

815
00:40:52,775 --> 00:40:54,735
The, uh, evidence tampering
is another matter

816
00:40:54,860 --> 00:40:57,112
but my attorney's gonna
work out a deal for probation.

817
00:40:57,237 --> 00:40:59,365
Well, that's...
that's very good for Jackson.

818
00:40:59,531 --> 00:41:02,493
Yeah. I'm told that somebody
high up in your office

819
00:41:02,618 --> 00:41:04,954
was very influential
with the DA.

820
00:41:05,621 --> 00:41:07,915
Mm. Wasn't me.
Good to see you.

821
00:41:08,040 --> 00:41:09,750
Yeah. But I brought you
a gift anyway.

822
00:41:09,959 --> 00:41:13,504
- Oh, listen, thank you but I-I can't...
- Please?

823
00:41:14,838 --> 00:41:15,881
Please.

824
00:41:16,382 --> 00:41:17,424
Okay.

825
00:41:19,134 --> 00:41:20,719
- Should I open it now?
- No.

826
00:41:27,101 --> 00:41:28,143
Was it worth it?

827
00:41:28,978 --> 00:41:30,020
Was what worth it?

828
00:41:30,479 --> 00:41:32,648
The man that killed your wife
no longer exists.

829
00:41:33,857 --> 00:41:35,734
The time you took pursuing him

830
00:41:37,069 --> 00:41:39,029
the pain, the sacrifice...
was it worth it?

831
00:41:41,031 --> 00:41:42,074
Yes, it was.

832
00:41:47,329 --> 00:41:48,372
Thank you.

